# feature-nilpy-str-surface-gaps-2026-08-09 — isnumeric() and istitle()
#
# Both were missing and both were LOUD: the compiler named them and listed the
# 36 str methods it does have, so neither could produce a wrong answer. They are
# the last two of that sweep's list.
#
# istitle is the one with real content. Titlecase means every RUN of letters
# starts uppercase and continues lowercase, with at least one cased character —
# so `"Title Case"` is True, `"Title case"` is False, `"A1b"` is False (the run
# resumes after the digit, and `b` does not start it uppercase), and a string
# with no letters at all is False rather than vacuously True.
#
# isnumeric delegates to isdigit: over the byte range pxx strings occupy, the
# two answers are identical, and they diverge only on Unicode characters with no
# representation here. It is its own routine rather than a call-site alias so
# that divergence has one place to be fixed when wide strings arrive.
#
# Every case is asserted against the NEIGHBOURING predicates too — isdigit,
# isalnum, isupper, islower — because these six share the "empty string is
# False" and "needs at least one cased character" rules, and a new one added by
# copying its neighbour is exactly how one of them ends up disagreeing.
#
# Expectations are CPython's own output.

cases = ["12", "", "ab1", "AB", "Hello, World", "Hello World", "A1b", "hello", "HELLO",
         "Title Case", "Title case", "123", "12.3", "  ", "A", "a", "AbC", "A-B", "a1B"]
for c in cases:
    print(repr(c), c.isnumeric(), c.istitle(), c.isdigit(), c.isalnum(), c.isupper(), c.islower())
